===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtɪt fə(ɹ) ˈtæt/|a=RP}}
*{{IPA|en|/ˈtɪt fɚ ˈtæt/|/ˈtɪt fɝ ˈtæt/|a=GA,CA}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-tit for tat.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{m|en|tit|t=a light blow or hit}}. Compare earlier {{m|en|tip for tap|t=(a) blow for (a) blow}}, from {{m|en|tip}} + {{m|en|tap}}; compare also dialectal {{m|en|tint for tant}}. Origin uncertain; conjectured to be ultimately from {{der|en|nl|[[dit]] [[voor]] [[dat]]||this for that}} or {{der|en|fr|[[tant]] [[pour]] [[tant]]||equal for equal}}.<ref>{{cite-book|entry=tit|editors=E[lizabeth] M[cLaren] Kirkpatrick and C[atherine] M. Schwarz|title=The Wordsworth Dictionary of Idioms|series=Wordsworth Reference Series|location=Ware, Hertfordshire|publisher=Wordsworth Reference|year=1993|page=391|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=8C7k7ZW3dIEC&pg=PA391|isbn=978-1-85326-309-5}}.</ref> The vowel sequence follows a typical [[w:Apophony#Ablaut-motivated compounding|ablaut reduplication]] pattern.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} [[equivalent#Adjective|Equivalent]] [[retribution]]; an act of [[return#Verb|returning]] [[exactly]] what one gets; an [[eye for an eye]].
#: {{ux|en|If you hit me, I’ll hit you back; '''tit for tat'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Robert Axelrod|title=The evolution of cooperation|page=13|passage='''Tit for tat''' is the policy of cooperating on the first move and then doing whatever the other player did on the previous move. This policy means that '''tit for tat''' will defect once after each defection of the other player.|pageurl=https://books.google.fr/books?id=SwU4DgAAQBAJ|isbn= 978-0465005642}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Ned Ward|[Edward Ward]]]|title=The Dutch Riddle: Or, A Character of a Hairy Monster, often Found in Holland, &c.|location=[London?]|publisher=Sold by the booksellers of London and Westminster|year=c. 1706|page=2|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=hbZaAAAAcAAJ&pg=PP8|oclc=66791273|passage=Could it but for a longer Space / Lengthen the Bliſs it let's us taſte, / Who would not [[doat]] on't? But alas! / The Joy's too exquiſite to laſt. / Two white ''Herculean'' Pillars prop / The tufted Gin, the tempting Snare; / When they divide, then in we pop, / Before we well know where we are. / Then that for this, and '''tit for tat'''; / But when the pleaſing Minute's flown, / As uſeleſs it returns the Bait, / And both look fooliſh when 'tis done.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Henry Carey (writer)|[Henry] Carey]]|title=The Honest Yorkshire-man. A Ballad Farce. As It is Acted at the Theatre-Royal|location=Dublin|publisher=Printed and sold by Thomas Wilkinson, in Winetavern Street, at the corner of Cook-Street; [...]|year=c. 1775|page=23|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=-xdgAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA23|oclc=723131215|passage=This is but '''Tit for Tat''', young Gentleman. Your Father wanted to get my Eſtate from me; and I have got the Wife he intended for you. All's fair, Sir.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Philip Wharton|chapter=II|title=Heart or Head. [...] In Two Volumes|location=London|publisher=Charles J. Skeet, 10, [[w:King William Street, London|King William Street]], {{w|Charing Cross}}|year=1864|volume=II|page=59|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/heartorheadbyph01thomgoog#page/n65/mode/1up|oclc=12121615|passage="You'll never get her over like that. You must coax her." / "Coax the devil. Do you think I'm going to be beat by a horse?" / "It would only be '''tit for tat'''; you beat her, so she has a right to beat you."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Georges Feydeau|tlr=Norman R. Shapiro|chapter=Hortense Said, ‘No Skin Off My Ass!’|editor=Maurice Charney|title=Comedy: New Perspectives|series=New York Literary Forum|seriesvolume=1|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=New York Literary Forum|month=spring|year=1978|page=307|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=51C0AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA307|isbn=978-0-931196-00-3|issn=0149-1040|passage=YVETTE. I have no idea what to make Monsieur for dinner. / FOLLBRAGUET. ''(beside himself)'' Well, that's no skin off my ass, damn it! / YVETTE. ''(snippily, giving him '''tit for tat''')'' Well, it's certainly no skin off mine, Monsieur!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:William M. LeoGrande; w:Peter Kornbluh|chapter=[[w:Bill Clinton|Clinton]]: From Calibrated Response to Parallel Positive Steps|title=Back Channel to Cuba: The Hidden History of Negotiations between Washington and Havana|location=Chapel Hill, N.C.|publisher=w:University of North Carolina Press|year=2014|page=279|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=zOZjBAAAQBAJ&pg=PA279|isbn=978-1-4696-1763-3|passage=As part of the planning for a new initiative on Cuba, the NSC &#91;[[w:United States National Security Council|National Security Council]]&#93; also revived the concept of "calibrated response." A new staffer was tasked to develop a list of "'''tit-for-tats'''" with Cuba—which sanctions might be lifted or softened in response to clear steps toward democracy by the [[w:Fidel Castro|[Fidel] Castro]] regime.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Arjun Kharpal|title=China Warns of ‘Tit for Tat’ on iPhone Sales if Trump Starts Trade War|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/tech-news/china-warns-tit-tat-iphone-sales-if-trump-starts-trade-n683446|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170908001249/https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/tech-news/china-warns-tit-tat-iphone-sales-if-trump-starts-trade-n683446|archivedate=8 September 2017|section=title|work=w:NBC News|date=14 November 2016|passage=China Warns of ‘'''Tit for Tat'''’ on iPhone Sales if Trump Starts Trade War}}

